# Decisions Log

## 2026-07-28 17:35:00 — Codex
Decision: Run the larger Vocalinux comparison with the English-specific quantized
`medium.en-q5_0` model.
Rationale: It is materially larger than `small.en-q5_1` while its 539 MB verified binary and
approximately 1.0 GB loaded footprint remain practical on this 15 GB Intel Vulkan host. The
full 1.46 GB medium model is unjustified while the root filesystem is already 98% utilized, and
candidate 3's main failure was segmentation rather than raw provider availability.

## 2026-07-28 17:29:00 — Codex
Decision: Rank Vocalinux as the offline/privacy fallback rather than the default noisy-room
dictation path, and advance the benchmark to raw browser Deepgram dictation without LLM polish.
Rationale: Vocalinux achieved exact lexical recognition but emitted a false environmental-sound
annotation and required about 3.9 seconds from silence boundary through inference. The browser
route bypasses the NoMachine virtual microphone, retains Deepgram `nova-3`, and avoids both the
continuous system microphone listener and post-STT model latency.

## 2026-07-28 17:27:00 — Codex
Decision: Test Vocalinux with `small.en-q5_1` instead of its prior `tiny.en-q5_1` configuration,
using the existing double-Ctrl toggle and a transient non-enabled service.
Rationale: The larger quantized English model is already installed and provides a fairer offline
accuracy comparison against Deepgram `nova-3`. Keeping activation operator-controlled avoids
the continuously open microphone behavior that produced unrelated background segments during
the first candidate test.

## 2026-07-28 17:25:00 — Codex
Decision: Use 60% internal-microphone gain for the current Deepgram dictation benchmark and
treat `Ctrl+Space` strictly as an edge-triggered mute toggle, not push-to-talk.
Rationale: At 27% the operator's speech did not produce a transcript, while a 100% diagnostic
capture reached the provider but peaked at clipping. Sixty percent restores threshold margin
without preserving the clipped signal. Independent X11 injection succeeded, so changing the
typing implementation would not address the observed failure.

## 2026-07-28 17:15:00 — Codex
Decision: Benchmark the NoMachine Deepgram `nova-3` dictation daemon first, Vocalinux second,
and browser Deepgram dictation third; evaluate `n-voice` plus Pipecat separately as a
conversational Voice-to-Nova stack rather than as a standalone transcription engine.
Rationale: The NoMachine daemon is the smallest already-tuned path for the operator's current
desktop, has 6,863 prior transcript segments, direct X11 typing, conservative spoken-command
handling, and no LLM post-processing latency. Vocalinux is the privacy-first offline fallback,
but its current `tiny.en-q5_1` model is a lower-accuracy baseline even though larger local models
are installed. `n-voice` owns routing, transcripts, memory, and agent delivery, while Pipecat
adds the full speech-to-agent-to-speech path; enabling either would confound a pure STT test.

## 2026-06-04 09:27:33 — Veyra, CommsOps - Tier 1 lead
Decision: Treat `wish` and `voice` as durable equivalent control words and bias Deepgram toward the exact local command vocabulary.
Rationale: The operator is dictating through NoMachine and needs short hands-free controls to survive realistic STT variants. Keyterm biasing improves recognition, while exact full-segment command matching still prevents ordinary prose such as `I would like to return later` from pressing Return.

## 2026-06-04 09:06:00 — Veyra, CommsOps - Tier 1 lead
Decision: Keep `wish` as a durable synonym for `voice` in local dictation commands and harden the toggle helper for terminals without inherited D-Bus environment variables.
Rationale: The operator wants `wish return` / `wish next line` as reliable hands-free controls, while local shell access should be able to pause or resume dictation without manually exporting `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` or `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS`. Deterministic `remote-stt-toggle on` and `remote-stt-toggle off` avoid ambiguity from the daemon's hotkey debounce.

## 2026-06-04 08:26:12 — Veyra, CommsOps - Tier 1 lead
Decision: Treat `wish` as the durable spoken control prefix for local dictation, equivalent to `voice`, while keeping all navigation/readback controls exact-match only.
Rationale: The operator wants hands-free control phrases such as `wish return` and `wish next line`, but prose containing `enter` or `return` must continue to type as text instead of firing unintended keypresses.

## 2026-06-04 07:58:00 — Veyra, CommsOps - Tier 1 lead
Decision: Use the daemon's own signal toggle to restore typing state instead of editing `/run/user/1000/remote-stt.state` directly.
Rationale: `SIGUSR1` exercises the same runtime path as the operator toggle and keeps the state transition consistent with the daemon's internal enabled flag.

## 2026-06-04 07:35:41 — Veyra, CommsOps - Tier 1 lead
Decision: Keep local dictation command handling conservative but recognition-tolerant, and make NoMachine STT restart into typing-on with quiet logs.
Rationale: The operator is actively using NoMachine dictation, so `wish` must behave as the same wake prefix as `voice` for navigation/readback commands. Those commands still need exact full-segment matching to avoid accidental keypresses from prose. The service state was stuck muted and debug logging was flooding skip messages, so defaulting the unit to typing-on and disabling skip debug makes the working path durable.

## 2026-06-04 06:58:11 — Veyra, CommsOps - Tier 1 lead
Decision: Use exact full-segment command matching for browser dictation navigation and readback, matching the NoMachine daemon.
Rationale: Browser dictation must not press Return from ordinary prose such as `I would like to return later`; `wish` remains a strict synonym for `voice` only when the segment is an intentional command.

## 2026-06-04 06:35:37 — Veyra, CommsOps - Tier 1 lead
Decision: Treat `wish` as a strict synonym for the `voice` command prefix, while keeping navigation words command-only when the normalized whole segment matches.
Rationale: The operator can say `wish return` or `wish next line` for control, but sentences such as `I would like to return later` must not press Return during dictation.

## 2026-06-04 06:08:03 — Veyra, CommsOps - Tier 1 lead
Decision: Treat selected-text readback as an exact spoken command in the local NoMachine dictation parser.
Rationale: Readback should be hands-free while remoted in through NoMachine, but it must not trigger from ordinary prose. Keeping readback as a full-segment command matches the safety model already used for `enter`, `return`, and `tab`.

## 2026-06-04 04:02:24 — Veyra, CommsOps - Tier 1 lead
Decision: Require full-segment matching for NoMachine dictation navigation keys while allowing inline spoken punctuation.
Rationale: `enter` and `return` should control the editor when spoken as commands, but normal phrases containing those words must remain text to avoid accidental line breaks during dictation.

## 2026-06-04 03:58:11 — Veyra, CommsOps - Tier 1 lead
Decision: Treat NoMachine dictation mute as a hard pause, not just a typing suppression switch.
Rationale: The NoMachine microphone can carry background room audio; skipping transcription while muted prevents accidental text floods, reduces API usage, and makes Ctrl+Space a reliable pause/resume control.

## 2026-06-04 03:53:01 — Veyra, CommsOps - Tier 1 lead
Decision: Restore NoMachine local dictation as a lightweight Deepgram-only daemon instead of the prior Deepgram plus local Whisper fallback path.
Rationale: The operator wants local desktop dictation while remoted in through NoMachine; lazy-loading Whisper and disabling fallback keeps the service around 35 MB at startup while preserving `xdotool` insertion into the active X11 session.

## 2026-06-04 03:30:16 — Veyra, CommsOps - Tier 1 lead
Decision: Keep the browser dictation service active as the low-latency path and leave `nomachine-remote-stt.service` disabled.
Rationale: The browser route verified in 622 ms with typing muted, while the NoMachine remote STT path previously showed failed fallback behavior and high memory consumption.

## 2026-06-04 03:29:05 — Veyra, CommsOps - Tier 1 lead
Decision: Favor raw Deepgram Nova-3 browser dictation over LLM-polished dictation for the active operator path.
Rationale: The configured 15-second polish read timeout directly matched the reported dictation lag, while raw Deepgram typing preserves the working browser-microphone bypass and avoids reintroducing the high-memory NoMachine Whisper fallback.

## 2026-05-28 06:42:23 — sable
Decision: Add browser-side audio diagnostics before changing providers.
Rationale: The server receives TTS PCM from Deepgram, so the next highest-leverage step is to distinguish silent browser/desktop output from missing agent audio with a local test tone and frame counters.

## 2026-05-28 05:37:09 — sable
Decision: Use the existing LLM proxy as a post-STT formatting pass instead of embeddings.
Rationale: Embeddings cannot directly repair punctuation or formatting; a low-temperature chat-completions rewrite can, and the dictation path can fall back to raw STT output if the provider is slow or unavailable.

## 2026-05-28 05:24:50 — sable
Decision: Implement voice mute as a typing-state toggle, not a microphone disconnect.
Rationale: The operator needs to speak freely without text going into the wrong box, while preserving low-friction recovery and avoiding microphone permission churn.

## 2026-05-28 05:17:36 — sable
Decision: Use `sable` as the operational signature for this voice/ops work.
Rationale: The operator explicitly requested replacing placeholder signatures with Sable's identity.

## 2026-05-28 05:13:25 — sable
Decision: Translate spoken editing and punctuation commands in the server-side dictation typer.
Rationale: Running command conversion after transcription but before `xdotool` insertion makes it deterministic and works across terminals, browser text boxes, and editors in the X11 session.

## 2026-05-28 05:07:10 — sable
Decision: Bias browser dictation toward complete utterances and key domain terms.
Rationale: Longer silence padding and shorter minimum speech windows address clipped endings and single-word misses, while Nova-3 `keyterm` prompting improves recognition for terms such as `autonomous`.

## 2026-05-28 03:11:25 — sable
Decision: Remove clipboard paste from browser dictation.
Rationale: Codex/browser targets treat clipboard paste as an image-capable operation and can emit missing-image clipboard errors; serialized direct typing avoids clipboard side effects.

## 2026-05-28 02:33:44 — sable
Decision: Disable the legacy NoMachine remote STT daemon while `/dictation` is the active dictation path.
Rationale: The daemon can be unmuted independently and then competes with browser dictation, causing duplicate or interleaved typed text.

## 2026-05-28 02:33:44 — sable
Decision: Paste completed dictation segments through the clipboard before falling back to direct `xdotool type`.
Rationale: Atomic paste is less prone to character interleaving and dropped keys than slow per-character typing.

## 2026-05-28 02:21:29 — sable
Decision: Add browser-microphone dictation as a bypass for NoMachine client microphone forwarding.
Rationale: NoMachine's virtual microphone path is silent after reboot, while browser microphone capture can still reach the server over HTTPS and type into the active desktop via `xdotool`.

## 2026-05-28 01:43:57 — sable
Decision: Retune NoMachine remote STT for low-latency dictation and preserve mute state across restarts.
Rationale: The voice services were running after reboot, Deepgram answered quickly, and the user-visible lag matched local 10-second batching rather than provider outage.

## 2025-11-28 21:45:00
- Decision: Use `xdotool` for typing if available, as it is the most reliable for X11.
- Decision: Suppress WebSocket 1000 closure "error" in logs as it is normal behavior.
